Then to all those gods who had surrendered, along with the sages and world-protectors, / Hari, together with Kālī, said: 'Go to the unswerving Soma. / Propitiate the Lord of the Gods, and peace will come to you.' / Sanatkumāra said: / Thus addressed by Hari, they went, overcome with anger [and grief], / composed, afflicted with suffering and sorrow, to propitiate the god. / Then, not far off, those great-souled ones, / their minds entirely focused on him, saw Īśvara accompanied by Ambā and his retinue. / Having seen him, bowing low, placing Brahmā at their head, / they praised the one of blazing splendour with sacrificial words. / The gods said: / 'Obeisance to you, slayer of the enemies of the gods, crusher of the gods' sacrifice, / O great sacrifice, O great being, praised by hundreds of great sacrifices! / Obeisance to the destroyer of the sacrifice, and the giver of the all of the Veda; / to the maker of the Śipiviṣṭa [form], the slayer of Viṣṇu's Narasiṃha [incarnation]; / to the dweller on Mount Mandara, the giver of every auspicious thing; / to him who travels in a great aerial chariot, the causer of hindrance to the seasons; / to the performer of the end of mantras, to the observer of mantra vows; / to the destroyer of Pūṣan's teeth, to the taker of Bhaga's eyes;

In some places it shone like a heap of collyrium, and with quartz crystal rock; / in some places with celestial golden rock, and again in some with multicolored rock;
in some places with a peak of red arsenic, in some resembling yellow orpiment; / [adorned] with śāla, tāla, tamāla, priyāla, and āmrātaka trees;
with aśoka, campaka, lodhra, kadamba, mango, and atimuktaka trees; / with nāga, punnāga, and tilaka trees, and also fragrant sandalwood trees;
with dhātakī and ketakī trees, and likewise with uddālaka trees; / with diverse plantains, date palms, and jackfruit trees;
adorned with bakula and coconut trees and clusters of lotuses; / with wood-apple, khadira, bhavya, and pārāvata trees;

accompanied by peacocks and cātakas, crowded with swans and cakravākas, / made to resound by cranes, wagtails, and green pigeons,
teeming with jīvañjīvaka birds, crowded with buffaloes and bears, / frequented by lions and tigers, and filled with śarabhas and elephants;
resembling Meru and Mandara, bestowing the fruit of all merit, / delightful, like heaven inaccessible to sinners of cruel deeds,
adorned with fortune like a true, honored pleasure-garden. / There, some were trees with honeyed fruits, granting all desires in all seasons;
others were lovely trees named kṣīrin; / other trees bore all flowers and fruits;
other trees flowered and bore fruit of gold; / others produced all kinds of edible dishes and foods everywhere;
great trees produced human and heavenly things of the six tastes; / others brought forth garments and ornaments among their fruits.
